IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `types`
IMPORT `chapter-2-explanations`
IMPORT `chapter-4-exceptions`
IMPORT `chapter-4a-private-defence`
IMPORT `chapter-17-property`

§ `Chapter 17 -- Misappropriation, aggravated criminal breach of trust, and stolen property`

-- Penal Code 1871 ss 404, 407, 408, 409, 410, 411, 412, 413, 414.
-- Source: registers/source-bundle/PC1871.txt.
--
-- Three small groups that share a subject. s 404 is misappropriation (s 403, already
-- encoded) of a dead person's property. ss 407 to 409 are criminal breach of trust
-- (s 405, already encoded) aggravated by the capacity in which the entrustment was
-- made. ss 410 to 414 are the receiving offences, and s 410's definition of "stolen
-- property" reaches back to theft, extortion, robbery, misappropriation, criminal
-- breach of trust and cheating -- which is why this module sits after all of them.
--
-- PUNISHMENT IS NOT COMPUTED. s 406 is the punishment section for criminal breach of
-- trust and is not encoded.

§§ `Section 404 -- Misappropriation of the property of a deceased person`

-- Dishonest misappropriation or conversion of property, knowing that it was in the
-- possession of a deceased person at the time of that person's decease and has not
-- since been in the possession of anyone legally entitled to it.
-- The heavier punishment where the offender was the deceased's clerk or servant is
-- punishment only, and the clerk-or-servant fact is already carried for s 381.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 404"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes dishonest misappropriation of a deceased person's property under Penal Code 1871 section 404
GIVEN t IS A `Property Taking` @desc Dishonest misappropriation of property known to have been in a deceased person's possession at death and not since in the possession of anyone legally entitled to it.
      f IS A Fault @desc Fault elements. The misappropriation must be dishonest under s 24.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes misappropriation of a deceased person property` t f IF
        `the act is done dishonestly` f
    AND t's `dishonestly misappropriates or converts movable property to own use`
    AND t's `knows that the property was in the possession of a deceased person at the time of that person decease and has not since been in the possession of any person legally entitled to it`

§§ `Sections 407, 408 and 409 -- Aggravated criminal breach of trust`

-- Each is s 405 criminal breach of trust plus the capacity in which the property was
-- entrusted: for transportation or storage for hire (s 407); to an employee (s 408);
-- or to a public servant, a banker, merchant, factor, broker, attorney or agent, a
-- professional, a company director, an officer of an unincorporated association, a
-- partner, a key executive or a fiduciary (s 409).
--
-- s 408(2) and s 409(2) and (3) widen the capacities: an employee includes a person
-- engaged in a capacity with the same fundamental qualities; a person may hold any of
-- the s 409 capacities without receiving remuneration; and a person may be a
-- fiduciary despite a contract stating that no fiduciary relationship arises. All of
-- those widen what may be asserted and add no limb, so each capacity is carried as a
-- single fact.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 407"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes criminal breach of trust of property entrusted for transportation or storage under Penal Code 1871 section 407
GIVEN tr IS A Trust @desc The s 405 facts, where the entrustment was of property for the purpose of transportation or storage for hire.
      f IS A Fault @desc Fault elements for the dishonest limbs of s 405.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes criminal breach of trust in transportation or storage` tr f IF
        `constitutes criminal breach of trust` tr f
    AND tr's `the entrustment was of property for the purpose of transportation or storage for hire`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 408"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes criminal breach of trust by an employee under Penal Code 1871 section 408
GIVEN tr IS A Trust @desc The s 405 facts, where the accused is an employee, or engaged in a capacity with the same fundamental qualities, and was entrusted in that capacity.
      f IS A Fault @desc Fault elements for the dishonest limbs of s 405.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes criminal breach of trust by an employee` tr f IF
        `constitutes criminal breach of trust` tr f
    AND tr's `the accused is an employee or engaged in a capacity with the same fundamental qualities as an employee and was entrusted in that capacity`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 409"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes criminal breach of trust by a public servant, banker, agent, director or fiduciary under Penal Code 1871 section 409
GIVEN tr IS A Trust @desc The s 405 facts, where the entrustment was in one of the capacities listed in s 409(1)(a) to (h).
      f IS A Fault @desc Fault elements for the dishonest limbs of s 405.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes criminal breach of trust in a special capacity` tr f IF
        `constitutes criminal breach of trust` tr f
    AND (   tr's `the accused was entrusted in his capacity as a public servant`
         OR tr's `the accused was entrusted in the way of his trade profession or business as a banker merchant factor broker attorney or agent`
         OR tr's `the accused was entrusted in his professional capacity otherwise than by way of such a trade profession or business`
         OR tr's `the accused was entrusted in his capacity as a director of a corporation an officer of an unincorporated association a partner in a partnership a key executive or a fiduciary`)

§§ `Section 410 -- Stolen property`

-- Property whose possession has been transferred by theft, extortion or robbery, or
-- which has been criminally misappropriated, or in respect of which criminal breach
-- of trust or cheating has been committed, is stolen property -- whether that
-- happened within or without Singapore.
--
-- Two things narrow and widen it. It CEASES to be stolen property if it subsequently
-- comes into the possession of a person legally entitled to it, which is a negated
-- conjunct. And s 410(2) extends it to anything into or for which stolen property has
-- been converted or exchanged, however remotely -- which is an alternative route, not
-- a condition, so it is disjoined.
--
-- The Explanation provides that the prosecution need not prove the elements of the
-- offence that made the property stolen. That removes a burden rather than adding an
-- element, so the underlying offence is carried as one asserted fact and is not
-- computed from the theft, extortion or cheating rules.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 410"
@export Decide whether property is stolen property within the meaning of Penal Code 1871 section 410
GIVEN sp IS A `Stolen Property` @desc Property transferred by theft, extortion or robbery, or misappropriated, or the subject of criminal breach of trust or cheating -- or anything it has been converted or exchanged into. It ceases to be stolen property once a person legally entitled to it has possession.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the property is stolen property` IF
        (   sp's `the possession of the property has been transferred by theft extortion or robbery or the property has been criminally misappropriated or is property in respect of which criminal breach of trust or cheating has been committed`
         OR sp's `the property is property into or for which stolen property has been converted or exchanged or anything acquired by such conversion or exchange`)
    AND (NOT sp's `the property has since come into the possession of a person legally entitled to the possession of it`)

§§ `Sections 411 to 414 -- Receiving, dealing in and concealing`

-- The four sections share a knowledge element -- knowing or having reason to believe
-- the property to be stolen property, or property obtained in whole or in part
-- through an offence involving fraud or dishonesty. Note that second branch: it is
-- WIDER than s 410, so a person may be guilty under s 411 in respect of property
-- that is not "stolen property" at all. The rules below therefore do not conjoin
-- `the property is stolen property`.
--
--   s 411  receiving or retaining
--   s 412  receiving or retaining what he knows was taken in a gang-robbery, or
--          receiving from someone he knows to be or have been a gang-robber
--   s 413  habitually receiving or dealing
--   s 414  voluntarily assisting in concealing, disposing of or making away with
--
-- ss 411(3) and 412(2) give a defence -- a reasonable excuse to receive or retain,
-- plus reasonable care as soon as practicable after acquiring the knowledge. Both
-- Illustrations are of a person who reports the property promptly. The defence is
-- given to ss 411 and 412 only; ss 413 and 414 do not have it, and the encoding does
-- not extend it to them.
-- The motor-vehicle subsections in ss 411(2) and 414(2) are punishment and
-- disqualification, and are not encoded.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 411 to 414"
GIVEN sp IS A `Stolen Property`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accused has the guilty knowledge` IF
    sp's `knows or has reason to believe the property to be stolen property or property obtained in whole or in part through an offence involving fraud or dishonesty`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 411(3) and 412(2)"
GIVEN sp IS A `Stolen Property`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the reasonable excuse defence is made out` IF
    sp's `the accused proves a reasonable excuse to receive or retain the property and that he exercised reasonable care as soon as practicable after acquiring that knowledge or reason to believe`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 411"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes receiving stolen property under Penal Code 1871 section 411
GIVEN sp IS A `Stolen Property` @desc Receiving or retaining property known or believed to be stolen or fraudulently obtained, where the s 411(3) reasonable-excuse defence is not made out.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes receiving stolen property` IF
        sp's `receives or retains the property`
    AND `the accused has the guilty knowledge` sp
    AND (NOT `the reasonable excuse defence is made out` sp)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 412"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes receiving property stolen in a gang-robbery under Penal Code 1871 section 412
GIVEN sp IS A `Stolen Property` @desc Receiving or retaining property known to have been taken in a gang-robbery, or received from a known gang-robber, where the s 412(2) defence is not made out.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes receiving property stolen in a gang-robbery` IF
        sp's `receives or retains the property`
    AND (   sp's `knows or has reason to believe the possession of the property to have been transferred by the commission of gang-robbery`
         OR sp's `receives the property from a person whom he knows or has reason to believe to belong or to have belonged to gang-robbers and knows or has reason to believe the property to have been stolen`)
    AND (NOT `the reasonable excuse defence is made out` sp)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 413"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes habitually dealing in stolen property under Penal Code 1871 section 413
GIVEN sp IS A `Stolen Property` @desc Habitually receiving or dealing in property known or believed to be stolen or fraudulently obtained. This section has no reasonable-excuse defence.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes habitually dealing in stolen property` IF
        sp's `habitually receives or deals in the property`
    AND `the accused has the guilty knowledge` sp

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 414"
@export Decide whether the act constitutes assisting in the concealment or disposal of stolen property under Penal Code 1871 section 414
GIVEN sp IS A `Stolen Property` @desc Voluntarily assisting in concealing, disposing of or making away with property known or believed to be stolen or fraudulently obtained. This section has no reasonable-excuse defence.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `constitutes assisting in the concealment of stolen property` IF
        sp's `voluntarily assists in concealing or disposing of or making away with the property`
    AND `the accused has the guilty knowledge` sp

§§ `Proposed Act wrappers`

GIVEN a IS A `Proposed Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the proposed act constitutes misappropriation of a deceased person property` IF
        `constitutes misappropriation of a deceased person property` (a's `property taking`) (a's `fault`)
    AND NOT (`the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or Chapter 4A` a)

GIVEN a IS A `Proposed Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the proposed act constitutes aggravated criminal breach of trust` IF
        (   `constitutes criminal breach of trust in transportation or storage` (a's `trust`) (a's `fault`)
         OR `constitutes criminal breach of trust by an employee` (a's `trust`) (a's `fault`)
         OR `constitutes criminal breach of trust in a special capacity` (a's `trust`) (a's `fault`))
    AND NOT (`the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or Chapter 4A` a)

GIVEN a IS A `Proposed Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the proposed act constitutes a stolen property offence` IF
        (   `constitutes receiving stolen property` (a's `stolen property`)
         OR `constitutes receiving property stolen in a gang-robbery` (a's `stolen property`)
         OR `constitutes habitually dealing in stolen property` (a's `stolen property`)
         OR `constitutes assisting in the concealment of stolen property` (a's `stolen property`))
    AND NOT (`the act is taken outside offence by Chapter 4 or Chapter 4A` a)
